WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.959
Here is a short program with a Turtle class.

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:06.619
First it declares a variable t

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.282
with no object yet.

00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:10.444
Then new Turtle creates a

00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:13.437
Turtle at 100, 100

00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:17.179
and t.jumpTo moves it to

00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:18.260
300, 200.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.009
Now we write: Turtle u = t

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:25.090
and we move u to 5, 5.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.975
Where is t now?

00:00:26.975 --> 00:00:29.840
Pause the video and decide.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.755
To answer that, we need classes and objects.

00:00:36.755 --> 00:00:38.574
A class is a blueprint.

00:00:38.574 --> 00:00:41.908
It declares the fields, which are the data

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:45.092
and the methods, which are the behavior

00:00:45.092 --> 00:00:47.820
that all objects of one kind share.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.196
An object is an instance of a class

00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:54.978
one concrete thing built from the blueprint

00:00:54.978 --> 00:00:58.004
with its own values for the fields.

00:00:58.004 --> 00:01:01.618
The class Dog has the fields name, color

00:01:01.618 --> 00:01:03.215
height and length

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:05.737
the object tommy has the name

00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:08.090
Tommy and the color green.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.993
Of the ideas of object-oriented programming

00:01:11.993 --> 00:01:14.820
this course uses one all the time

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.031
encapsulation.

00:01:16.031 --> 00:01:18.050
Keep the fields private

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.200
and let other classes use the methods.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.608
Here is the Turtle class from the slides.

00:01:24.608 --> 00:01:26.746
Its fields, penDown, x and y

00:01:26.746 --> 00:01:28.953
are declared in the class body

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:30.953
and every Turtle object gets

00:01:31.022 --> 00:01:32.471
its own copy of them.

00:01:32.471 --> 00:01:35.160
Statements, such as these assignments

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.299
can only appear inside methods

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:38.540
and constructors.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.722
This is the constructor.

00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:42.935
It initializes a new object

00:01:43.012 --> 00:01:45.607
and runs automatically right after

00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:47.897
new has allocated the memory.

00:01:47.897 --> 00:01:50.644
Its name is exactly the class name

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:53.850
and it has no return type, not even void.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.439
Writing a constructor is optional.

00:01:56.505 --> 00:01:58.744
A class without one gets a default

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.851
constructor with no parameters

00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:02.497
which leaves every field

00:02:02.563 --> 00:02:03.946
at its default value.

00:02:03.946 --> 00:02:06.712
But as soon as you write any constructor

00:02:06.712 --> 00:02:09.280
the default one is no longer provided.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.333
Java has three kinds of methods.

00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:14.420
The main method is static

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.743
it belongs to the class, not to an object

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.061
so the JVM can call it before

00:02:20.138 --> 00:02:21.530
any object exists.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.152
Its parameter, args

00:02:23.152 --> 00:02:25.780
holds the command-line arguments.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.965
The second kind is the constructor

00:02:28.965 --> 00:02:30.989
which we have just seen.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:34.634
And a general method has an access modifier

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:38.278
a return type, a name, a list of parameters

00:02:38.278 --> 00:02:39.250
and a body.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.521
getX() has no parameters and returns an int

00:02:43.521 --> 00:02:44.707
with return.

00:02:44.707 --> 00:02:48.719
jumpTo() takes two ints and returns nothing

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:51.273
so its return type is void

00:02:51.273 --> 00:02:54.100
it changes the object instead.

00:02:54.191 --> 00:02:56.927
A method that is not static is

00:02:57.018 --> 00:02:59.480
always called on an object.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.431
Who may use a field or a method

00:03:02.507 --> 00:03:04.801
is set by its access modifier.

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:06.789
Public means every class.

00:03:06.866 --> 00:03:09.313
Protected means the same package

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:10.842
and the subclasses.

00:03:10.842 --> 00:03:12.219
With no modifier

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:15.125
only the classes of the same package.

00:03:15.125 --> 00:03:18.260
And private means only the class itself.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:22.367
The field penDown is private, so this line

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:25.440
in another class, does not compile

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.770
penDown has private access in Turtle.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.992
Keep the fields private

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:32.943
and let other classes go

00:03:33.021 --> 00:03:35.751
through its methods, such as getX().

00:03:35.751 --> 00:03:37.546
This is encapsulation

00:03:37.546 --> 00:03:40.978
the class controls how its data can change.

00:03:40.978 --> 00:03:43.163
A linked list, for example

00:03:43.163 --> 00:03:46.049
keeps its head and its size private

00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:49.560
so that no outside code can break the links.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.170
Let's run it.

00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:53.784
Turtle t creates a variable that

00:03:53.863 --> 00:03:55.605
can refer to a Turtle

00:03:55.605 --> 00:03:57.823
but there is no object yet.

00:03:57.823 --> 00:04:00.675
new allocates a Turtle on the heap

00:04:00.675 --> 00:04:02.417
runs the constructor

00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:04.397
and returns a reference

00:04:04.397 --> 00:04:06.140
which is stored in t.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.530
getX() returns 100

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.414
which is copied into a

00:04:10.414 --> 00:04:12.297
and the program prints

00:04:12.297 --> 00:04:14.024
a equals 100.

00:04:14.102 --> 00:04:16.064
Then the jump changes the

00:04:16.143 --> 00:04:17.869
fields of that object,

00:04:17.869 --> 00:04:20.930
so t is at 300, 200.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.531
Now, Turtle u = t.

00:04:23.531 --> 00:04:27.738
This copies the reference, not the Turtle.

00:04:27.738 --> 00:04:31.457
u and t are two names for one object.

00:04:31.555 --> 00:04:34.099
So u.jumpTo moves the

00:04:34.197 --> 00:04:38.013
only Turtle there is, and t is at

00:04:38.013 --> 00:04:38.600
5, 5.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.216
Only new creates an object.

00:04:41.216 --> 00:04:44.070
Assigning a reference never does.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.598
How does that Turtle ever go away?

00:04:47.598 --> 00:04:49.332
Java has no delete.

00:04:49.332 --> 00:04:52.800
When main returns, its frame is popped

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.748
and t, a and u disappear with it.

00:04:55.748 --> 00:04:59.043
Now no variable refers to the Turtle,

00:04:59.043 --> 00:05:00.690
it is unreachable.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.353
The garbage collector frees its

00:05:03.427 --> 00:05:05.367
memory at some later time.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:07.978
You never destroy objects by hand,

00:05:07.978 --> 00:05:10.440
you just stop referring to them.

00:05:10.514 --> 00:05:12.677
This removes two classic bugs

00:05:12.752 --> 00:05:15.960
of C and C++: freeing memory twice

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.720
and using memory after it was freed.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.628
Inside a method or a constructor,

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:23.322
the keyword this refers

00:05:23.392 --> 00:05:24.944
to the current object,

00:05:24.944 --> 00:05:27.061
the object being constructed

00:05:27.061 --> 00:05:29.672
or the one the method was called on.

00:05:29.742 --> 00:05:32.282
Its main use is to tell a field from

00:05:32.353 --> 00:05:34.540
a parameter with the same name.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:38.344
In ItemWrong, the parameter is called name

00:05:38.344 --> 00:05:39.993
just like the field.

00:05:39.993 --> 00:05:41.877
Inside the constructor,

00:05:41.877 --> 00:05:44.389
every name means the parameter,

00:05:44.468 --> 00:05:46.587
so name = name assigns

00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:48.550
the parameter to itself.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.690
The field is never set, and stays null.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.005
In Item, this.name is the field

00:05:55.005 --> 00:05:57.228
and name is the parameter.

00:05:57.228 --> 00:06:00.440
So the demo prints null, and then car.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.759
Many things can go wrong

00:06:02.831 --> 00:06:05.657
while a program runs: division by zero,

00:06:05.657 --> 00:06:07.613
an index outside an array,

00:06:07.613 --> 00:06:10.294
a method called on a null reference,

00:06:10.294 --> 00:06:12.540
or input that is not a number.

00:06:12.612 --> 00:06:14.568
Java reports such a problem

00:06:14.641 --> 00:06:16.452
by throwing an exception,

00:06:16.452 --> 00:06:19.060
an object that describes the error.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.806
This method reads a natural

00:06:21.895 --> 00:06:23.759
number from a string.

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:27.664
An empty string throws a new IOException.

00:06:27.664 --> 00:06:31.481
So does any character that is not a digit.

00:06:31.481 --> 00:06:34.587
Otherwise, each digit is added on

00:06:34.587 --> 00:06:36.096
value * 10

00:06:36.096 --> 00:06:40.090
plus the character - '0'.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.920
Throwing stops the method at once

00:06:42.993 --> 00:06:44.816
and the exception travels

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:46.566
back up the call stack

00:06:46.566 --> 00:06:48.754
until some method catches it.

00:06:48.754 --> 00:06:51.525
IOException is a checked exception

00:06:51.525 --> 00:06:54.516
so the method must say so in its header

00:06:54.516 --> 00:06:56.120
throws IOException.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.233
The caller handles an exception

00:06:59.315 --> 00:07:00.868
with try and catch.

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:03.973
CatchDemo calls parse on two inputs

00:07:03.973 --> 00:07:06.261
21, and '4x2'.

00:07:06.343 --> 00:07:08.386
The statements in the try

00:07:08.467 --> 00:07:10.020
block run as usual.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:13.475
For 21, parse returns 21

00:07:13.475 --> 00:07:15.369
and m becomes 42.

00:07:15.369 --> 00:07:17.111
There is no exception

00:07:17.111 --> 00:07:19.459
so the catch block is skipped.

00:07:19.459 --> 00:07:21.807
The finally block runs anyway

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:25.215
and the program prints: n = 21

00:07:25.215 --> 00:07:26.730
m = 42.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:28.282
For '4x2'

00:07:28.282 --> 00:07:30.687
parse throws at the letter 'x'.

00:07:30.687 --> 00:07:33.733
The rest of the try block is skipped

00:07:33.733 --> 00:07:35.657
so m is never computed.

00:07:35.657 --> 00:07:38.142
The matching catch block runs

00:07:38.142 --> 00:07:39.985
it prints the message

00:07:39.985 --> 00:07:42.711
and uses 1 and 2 as defaults.

00:07:42.711 --> 00:07:45.276
Finally runs again, and we get

00:07:45.276 --> 00:07:47.520
n = 1, m = 2.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:51.124
So try runs until something throws

00:07:51.124 --> 00:07:55.215
catch runs only when the exception matches

00:07:55.215 --> 00:07:58.190
and finally runs in every case.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.399
Most exceptions you will meet in this

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.814
course are thrown by Java itself.

00:08:03.814 --> 00:08:05.235
They are unchecked

00:08:05.235 --> 00:08:07.650
so methods need not declare them.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.093
Index 3, in an array of length 3

00:08:11.093 --> 00:08:12.451
is out of bounds.

00:08:12.526 --> 00:08:15.317
Calling length on a null string gives

00:08:15.393 --> 00:08:17.278
a NullPointerException.

00:08:17.354 --> 00:08:19.390
Integer division by zero is

00:08:19.466 --> 00:08:21.276
an ArithmeticException

00:08:21.352 --> 00:08:23.539
but dividing a double by zero

00:08:23.615 --> 00:08:25.350
is not an error at all

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.160
the result is infinity.

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.798
If nobody catches an exception,

00:08:29.798 --> 00:08:33.045
the program stops and prints the exception

00:08:33.118 --> 00:08:34.594
and the stack trace.

00:08:34.668 --> 00:08:36.587
And never swallow one with

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:39.612
an empty catch block; at the very least,

00:08:39.612 --> 00:08:40.350
print it.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:41.771
To sum up.

00:08:41.771 --> 00:08:44.293
One: A class declares fields,

00:08:44.293 --> 00:08:46.478
constructors, and methods,

00:08:46.478 --> 00:08:49.757
and `new` creates an object on the heap.

00:08:49.757 --> 00:08:52.447
Two: A variable of a class type

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:54.044
holds a reference.

00:08:54.044 --> 00:08:57.071
Copying it never copies the object,

00:08:57.071 --> 00:09:00.013
and `this` names the current object.

00:09:00.013 --> 00:09:03.207
Three: `throw` stops a method, and `try`,

00:09:03.207 --> 00:09:06.570
`catch`, and `finally` handle the exception.

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.225
Try this yourself.

00:09:08.225 --> 00:09:10.256
Write a class `BankAccount`

00:09:10.256 --> 00:09:13.085
with a private `balance`, a constructor,

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.623
`deposit`, `withdraw`, and `getBalance` methods.

00:09:15.695 --> 00:09:18.524
`withdraw` must throw an `IllegalArgumentException`

00:09:18.596 --> 00:09:21.135
when the amount is larger

00:09:21.207 --> 00:09:22.440
than the balance.

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:27.109
In part four, we turn to arrays, strings,

00:09:27.109 --> 00:09:29.110
casting, and files.

